.TH TANGSIBLE-RUN 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible-run \- run a playbook with a live, navigable tree UI
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible run
.RI [ playbook.yml ]
.RI [ \-\-start\-at\-play
.IR name ]
.RI [ "ansible\-playbook args" ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run
starts
.I playbook.yml
under
.BR ansible-playbook (1)
and shows its progress as a live tree of plays \(-> tasks \(-> hosts,
updating incrementally as the run proceeds. Any trailing arguments are
passed straight through to
.B ansible\-playbook
unchanged (inventory, tags, limit, extra\-vars, verbosity,
.BR \-\-start\-at\-task ,
and so on all continue to work exactly as they would calling
.B ansible\-playbook
directly) \(em with one exception,
.BR \-\-start\-at\-play ,
covered in STARTING PARTWAY THROUGH below.
.B \-\-diff
is appended automatically to every invocation, whether or not it was
already given, so the drill\-down view's Diff tab always has something
to show.
.PP
If
.I playbook.yml
is omitted, it is resolved as described in
.BR tangsible (1)'s
CONFIGURATION section. If no playbook can be resolved at all,
.B run
exits with a usage error.
.PP
A pre\-flight failure \(em a bad playbook path, a parse error, a missing
inventory \(em is reported directly to stderr, exactly as plain
.B ansible\-playbook
would; the TUI itself is never shown for a run that fails before its
first real event.
.SH STARTING PARTWAY THROUGH
.TP
.BI \-\-start\-at\-task " name"
A real
.BR ansible-playbook (1)
flag, passed straight through like any other. Skips ahead to the first
task named
.I name
and runs to the end from there. Since task names are not required to be
unique \(em most playbooks are thin wrappers around roles, and a role's
own first task tends to be named the same way across every play that
uses it \(em this can't always target one exact position by itself; see
.B \-\-start\-at\-play
below for a way to narrow it further.
.TP
.BI \-\-start\-at\-play " name"
Tangsible's own flag, understood by no real
.BR ansible-playbook :
it is stripped out of the argument list before anything else touches
it, so it is never passed through and never recorded into
.BR .tangsible/state.toml \(aqs
invocation history. Rather than editing
.I playbook.yml
itself, Tangsible builds a throwaway copy trimmed to start at the named
top\-level play \(em including everything after it \(em in the same
directory as the original file (so any
.BR roles: / include_tasks / import_tasks
reference still resolves exactly as it would have), runs
.B ansible\-playbook
against that copy instead, and removes it once the run finishes. Only a
playbook's own top\-level plays can be targeted this way; a play pulled
in via
.B import_playbook
or one with no
.B name:
at all cannot be. If no play named
.I name
exists in
.IR playbook.yml ,
.B run
fails immediately with a clear error and a non\-zero exit, before
anything is recorded to history and before
.B ansible\-playbook
is ever invoked \(em the same posture taken when no playbook can be
resolved at all.
.PP
The two combine: giving both
.B \-\-start\-at\-play
and
.B \-\-start\-at\-task
first trims the file down to the named play, then starts partway into
it at the named task \(em useful when a task name that would otherwise
be ambiguous across the whole playbook is unique within one play.
Both are also available as fields in the rerun dialog (\c
.BR r ,
below, or
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)),
autocompleted from every top\-level play/task name found in
.IR playbook.yml .
.B \-\-start\-at\-play
is also accepted directly on the
.B rerun
command line, to pre\-fill that dialog's own Play field \(em see
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
for how its handling there differs slightly from
.BR run 's.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
Full reference:
.IR Keyboard-shortcuts.md
in the project's
.B design-docs
directory. The essentials:
.TP
.BR "Up/Down " "or " "j/k"
Move the cursor (stops autoscrolling).
.TP
.B Left / Right
Collapse / expand the task under the cursor.
.TP
.B E / C
Expand / collapse every task at once.
.TP
.B n / N
Jump to the next / previous task.
.TP
.B F
Resume autoscrolling to the newest row.
.TP
.B f
Open the filter dialog: All / Interesting / Changed / Failed.
.TP
.B /
Search filter (task name, source, and output).
.TP
.B Enter
Toggle a task's host rows, or open a host's full result if the cursor
is on one.
.TP
.B r
Open the rerun dialog, once the run has finished. See
.BR tangsible-rerun (1)
for what it does.
.TP
.B d
Open the diff picker: choose a previous run to compare this one
against. Requires at least one other completed run recorded in
.BR .tangsible/state.toml ;
otherwise this key does nothing. See
.BR tangsible-revisit (1),
whose own list picker this reuses.
.TP
.B q / Ctrl\-C
While the playbook is still running, interrupt it (forwarded as
.BR SIGINT ,
exactly as pressing Ctrl\-C on a plain
.B ansible\-playbook
invocation would). Once it has finished, quit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
See
.BR tangsible (1).
